**Clarify.** The usual solution tracks two scalars, `max1` and `max2`. The constraint tells us to hold that state inside a data structure instead. Also ask: should the second max be the second *distinct* value? What if the array has fewer than 2 elements?

**Approach 1: bounded min-heap of size 2.** Use a `PriorityQueue` (min-heap). For each element, push it; if the heap size exceeds 2, pop the smallest. After one pass the heap contains the two largest values: the root is the second max, the other element is the max. Each operation touches at most 3 elements, so the total is O(n) time and O(1) space (the heap is bounded). For distinct values, skip the push when the value is already in the heap.

**Approach 2: TreeSet capped at size 2.** Add each element, and if size is greater than 2 remove `first()`. A set naturally ignores duplicates, which gives the distinct second maximum for free. Time O(n log 2) = O(n).

**Approach 3: fixed-size array of length 2** used as the two slots, updated with the same comparisons as the classic algorithm. This is the "data structure" form of the two-variable method.

**Sketch (Java):**

    PriorityQueue<Integer> pq = new PriorityQueue<>();
    for (int x : arr) {
        if (pq.contains(x)) continue;   // distinct values only
        pq.add(x);
        if (pq.size() > 2) pq.poll();
    }
    // pq.peek() is second max; the other element is max

(`contains` on a size-2 heap is O(1) in practice.)

**Edge cases:** all equal elements (no second distinct max), a single element, negative numbers, empty input. Compare with sorting at O(n log n) and two passes, and note why this is better for streaming data where the input does not fit in memory.
